This file credits third-party code and data that ameriflux-mcp ports, derives from or
vendors (DESIGN.md D27). Each entry is added, with the full upstream licence text, by
the build step that brings the material into the repository.

Current contents
----------------

1. amerifluxr (https://github.com/chuhousen/amerifluxr), BSD-3-Clause.
   src/ameriflux_mcp/ameriflux/variables/grammar.py classifies BASE variable names
   (gap-filled, PI-provided, quadruplet, replicate and layer aggregates, _SD, _N, fetch)
   following the rules of amerifluxr's amf_parse_basename (R/amf_parse_basename.R). The
   implementation is new (a tokenizer, not a translation of the R code), but it follows
   that function's design, so its notice is reproduced here.

2. ONEFlux (https://github.com/fluxnet/ONEFlux), LBNL BSD-3-Clause with the Enhancements
   clause. src/ameriflux_mcp/resources_static/fluxnet-variables.json records, per
   variable, the resolutions and timestamp formats defined in
   oneflux/metadata/var_info_definitions_{hh,dd,ww,mm,yy}.txt at commit
   d4ecebef96b83b33c82c8c0449aad308adf604cd (built by
   scripts/build_variable_dictionaries.py).

3. FLUXNET2015 variable dictionaries
   (https://ftp.fluxdata.org/.fluxnet_downloads_67934/webdoc/variable_codes_FULLSET_20200504.csv,
   ETag "10afb-5a4e12f9d7b40"; …/variable_codes_SUBSET_20200504.csv, ETag
   "48fd-5a4e12bcceb40"; fetched 2026-10-24). No licence is stated, so only a derived
   table (names, descriptions, units and notes per resolution, SUBSET membership) is
   committed, in fluxnet-variables.json, with these sources recorded in the file
   (licence question Q7 in research/questions.md). The original files are not
   redistributed.

4. AmeriFlux FP Standard variable list (https://amfcdn.lbl.gov/api/v1/fp_var?limits=True,
   fetched 2026-10-24): a snapshot in resources_static/fp-variables.json, used only when
   the live list cannot be fetched.

5. Verbatim policy text: the acknowledgment, data-availability and processing-reference
   sentences in src/ameriflux_mcp/ameriflux/acknowledgments.py are quoted from
   https://ameriflux.lbl.gov/data/data-policy/ and https://fluxnet.org/data/data-policy/
   (checked 2026-10-24) so that users reproduce them exactly.

6. FLUXNET Shuttle (https://github.com/fluxnet/shuttle), BSD-3-Clause.
   src/ameriflux_mcp/ameriflux/files/filenames.py uses the Shuttle's FLUXNET zip-name
   pattern (_FLUXNET_ZIP_PATTERN, src/fluxnet_shuttle/shuttle.py, checked 2026-10-24)
   verbatim; its licence is reproduced below.

7. tests/data/AMF_US-CRT_BASE_HH_2-5.csv, from amerifluxr's inst/extdata, is AmeriFlux
   data under CC-BY-4.0 (not BSD): Jiquan Chen, Housen Chu, AmeriFlux BASE US-CRT
   Curtice Walter-Berger cropland, Ver. 2-5 (outdated; current is Ver. 5-5), AmeriFlux
   AMP, (Dataset). https://doi.org/10.17190/AMF/1246156. Funding for the AmeriFlux data
   service was provided by the U.S. Department of Energy Office of Science. The sample's
   BIF workbooks are not vendored (they hold personal contact data); tests build
   synthetic ones instead.

Test fixtures in tests/fixtures/ are recorded AmeriFlux, AmeriFlux file-server, amerifluxr
and DataCite responses, cut to a few sites and with every email address replaced by a
synthetic example.org address (scripts/record_fixtures.py records each transform).

The research notes (RESEARCH.md, research/) quote short passages from public AmeriFlux and
FLUXNET web pages, data-policy text and API responses for reference, with their sources.

